// THE OPCODES THE NATIVE BACKEND STILL REFUSES, SPLIT OUT SO THEIR REFUSAL DOES
// NOT COST THE LOWERING HALF ITS EXECUTION.
//
// This file exists for a measured reason, and it is the SECOND time the reason
// has appeared. `module_refusals` reports per chunk, but the differential
// exempts per MODULE: one refusing chunk takes the whole file out of the
// comparison. So `opcode_witness.kel` lowered `Add`, `Sub`, `Mul`, `Neg`,
// `BoundsCheck`, `CheckedDiv`, `CheckedMod` and `CallExternalNative` while
// EXECUTING NONE OF THEM -- a lowering figure with no correctness behind it,
// which is the distinction this line keeps turning on.
//
// The float half moved out for the same reason one increment earlier, when a
// single float constant refused the module and took four unrelated opcodes'
// witnesses with it. THE FIX BELONGS IN THE CORPUS, NOT IN A GUARD: weakening a
// correct refusal to recover coverage is the trade this line refuses.
//
// WHAT IT WITNESSES, and why each is still refused:
//
//   FixedDiv   runtime-fault lowering, DEFERRED TO V0.4.0 by RUNTIME_FAULTS.md.
//              It returns DivisionByZero unconditionally with no construct to
//              catch it.
//   Len        reachable only through an `if` EXPRESSION as the for-in source,
//              and THAT PROGRAM CANNOT BE GIVEN A RESOURCE BOUND -- the loop has
//              no statically extractable iteration count. Reachable in bytecode,
//              not in an admissible program.
//   IsStruct   an UNANNOTATED struct-parameter pattern. Its witness LOADS and
//              then TRAPS on InvalidBytecode, which is a load-time hole rather
//              than a bad program. Blocked on an ownership question about
//              `src/verify.rs` that belongs to the operator.
//
// THIS FILE IS EXPECTED TO SIT IN THE DIFFERENTIAL'S EXEMPT SET, and it cannot
// be given an arena at all while `len_witness` is present. Do not "fix" that:
// the whole point is to concentrate the inadmissible parts in one place so the
// rest of the corpus can run.
//
// WITNESSES: FixedDiv, Len, IsStruct, FixedMul, WordToFixed, FixedToWord
//
// THE LINE ABOVE IS A MACHINE-CHECKED CLAIM, not a comment.
// `witness_integrity.rs` compiles this file and asserts every opcode named
// there is actually emitted. A witness that silently STOPS witnessing --
// because the reference compiler folded the construct away, or because a
// function moved to another file -- would otherwise drop a census figure
// with nothing going red.
//
// That is not hypothetical: the `Op::IsStruct` construct was repaired out of
// existence by the `v0.2.3` line one hour after this corpus adopted it.

// GENERIC on purpose -- see `is_struct_witness` below.
struct P<T> { a: T, b: T }


fn fixed_mix(w: Word) -> Word {
  let x = w as Fixed<16>;
  let two = 2 as Fixed<16>;
  let scaled = x * two;
  let halved = scaled / two;
  halved as Word
}


// `Op::IsStruct` — THE LAST UNWITNESSED OPCODE, and the construct is the
// `v0.2.3` line's after nine failed attempts of their own.
//
// It fires only when `named_type_name(ty) != Some(pattern_type)`, so it needs
// the pattern's type to be UNKNOWN at the test. An UNANNOTATED struct-parameter
// pattern does it; annotating the parameter folds the test out, which is the
// control their test asserts.
//
// AND IT IS A DIFFERENT CASE FROM `Op::Len`, WHICH IS WHY BOTH ARE HERE.
// Measured on this tree rather than taken from their report:
//
//   witness        verify()   module_wcmu   arena     load     run
//   Op::Len        accepts    REFUSES       REFUSED   n/a      never runs
//   Op::IsStruct   accepts    accepts       OK        LOADS    TRAPS
//
// `Op::Len`'s witness cannot be ADMITTED -- the strict iteration-bound check
// refuses it before it can load, which is the conservative-verification stance
// working as designed. `Op::IsStruct`'s witness satisfies EVERY load-time check,
// receives a memory bound, loads, and then dies at call time with
// `InvalidBytecode("Op::IsStruct on a flat struct; the type test is a
// compile-time constant")`.
//
// THAT SECOND ONE IS NOT THE SAME KIND OF FACT. `InvalidBytecode` is the class
// `verify()` exists to exclude AT LOAD TIME, so a legal program reaching it at
// RUN time is a load-time hole rather than a bad program. `src/verify.rs` is
// read-only to BOTH lines, so neither has repaired it and it is recorded for the
// operator. See the handoff.
// **THE WITNESS IS THE GENERIC INSTANTIATION, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.**
//
// The original witness was an UNANNOTATED struct parameter. The `v0.2.3` line
// repaired the load-time hole behind it by folding the irrefutable type test
// away, which is the right repair -- and it stopped this file emitting the
// opcode. `witness_integrity.rs` caught that on the first run after absorption,
// naming the file and the opcode, which is what it exists for.
//
// MEASURED AFTER THE FOLD: `Op::IsStruct` is NOT producerless. A GENERIC struct
// destructured in a parameter still emits it, still verifies, still takes a
// bound, still loads, and still dies with
// `InvalidBytecode("Op::IsStruct on a flat struct")`. The non-generic control
// returns `Int(3)`, so the repair works for exactly the case it was written for.
//
// So the witness moves to the form that SURVIVES the repair. It emits on both
// trees -- before the fold and after -- which is why it can be adopted here
// ahead of absorbing that change.
fn is_struct_witness(p: P<Word>) -> Word {
  destructure(p)
}


// The UNANNOTATED pattern is what leaves the type unknown at the test. Writing
// `fn destructure(p: P)` here folds `Op::IsStruct` out and the witness is lost.
// ANNOTATED with the generic instantiation. An un-annotated parameter is the
// construct the fold now covers; this one it does not.
fn destructure(P { a, b }: P<Word>) -> Word {
  a + b
}


// `Op::Len` — AND THE CONSTRUCT CAME FROM THE V0.2.X LINE, not from this one.
//
// `Len` fires only when the for-in source has NO STATICALLY KNOWN LENGTH, so
// reaching it needs INFERENCE TO FAIL rather than an unusual syntax. Between the
// two lines, FOURTEEN guessed constructs failed. What worked was not a fifteenth
// guess: it was reading `static_for_in_length`'s own match arms for what they
// OMIT. They handle ArrayLiteral, Call, FieldAccess, Ident, ArrayIndex and Match,
// then fall through to `_ => None`. `Expr::If` is not among them.
//
// THE METHOD IS THE TRANSFERABLE PART. When a fallback path resists probing,
// read the guard's arm list for the omission instead of enumerating constructs
// against it.
//
// `Op::IsStruct` is the remaining unwitnessed opcode and the identical trick
// does NOT reach it: its guard reads a type from `infer_expr_type`, which has no
// `Expr::If` arm either, yet the test still folds out. Making inference fail is
// NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT there, and what suppresses it is not established.
// Nine attempts are recorded in the `v0.2.3` line's `tests/opcode_reachability.rs`
// so a tenth does not repeat them. NOT written as unreachable by either line.
//
// THE BODY DISCARDS, and that is the corpus idiom rather than laziness: local
// bindings are immutable, so accumulation across iterations is not expressible
// in an atomic-total `fn` body at all. `04_for_in.kel` says so and uses the same
// shape. A first draft here carried `let mut total` and would not have compiled.
//
// THIS FUNCTION MAKES THE WHOLE MODULE INADMISSIBLE, AND THAT IS NOT A MISTAKE.
// `verify()` accepts it, but `auto_arena_capacity_for` REFUSES it: the loop "has
// no statically extractable iteration bound". The two facts are ONE fact.
// `Op::Len` fires exactly when the for-in source has no statically known length,
// and a loop whose trip count is not statically known is exactly what the bound
// extractor refuses. So the property that makes the opcode reachable is the
// property that makes the loop unbounded.
//
// MEASURED, ruling out the obvious objection: giving both arms the SAME length
// makes the trip count two on every path and provable by inspection, and it is
// STILL refused, because neither the length guard nor the bound extractor looks
// THROUGH an `Expr::If`. Second-category conservative rejection, not first.
//
// CONSEQUENCE FOR THIS FILE: it can never be given an arena while this function
// is present, so it can never leave the differential's exempt set by lowering
// alone. `probe_len_reachability.rs` pins that, including the harness panic that
// would follow if all four refused opcodes were lowered without adding a
// bound-refusal exemption class first. Read that file before "fixing" this one.
fn len_witness(c: bool) -> Word {
  let a = [1, 2];
  let b = [3, 4];
  for x in if c { a } else { b } {
    let _d = x;
  }
  0
}

fn main(seed: Word) -> Word {
  let q = fixed_mix(seed);
  let s = is_struct_witness(P { a: 1, b: 2 });
  let l = len_witness(true);
  q + s + l
}
